We need to produce a decision-complete implementation plan for boot-time restore implementation. The plan should be concrete for another engineer to implement directly, including failure handling and tests.

We have the snapshot of files: GameRuntimeServer, stateStore, replay, types, and a test file.

Goal: implement boot-time restore from persisted session state. The GameRuntimeServer already has a restoreSession method that attempts to load saved state from StateStore and reconstruct simulation, threads, etc. However, we need to ensure boot-time restore works correctly: when the server starts, it should attempt to restore a previous session (including simulation state, thread IDs, metrics, etc.) and continue from there, handling failures (e.g., missing state, corrupted state, mismatched model, etc.) and ensure tests cover this.

We need to produce a plan: steps, modifications to code, error handling, tests.

We need to be concrete: which files to edit, what to add, where to add try/catch, what to log, etc.

We need to consider that restoreSession currently loads saved state and sets simulation, seed, threadIds, etc., but there may be missing steps: need to ensure that after restore, we need to resume threads with Codex (resume thread), ensure that pending turns are requeued, ensure that the scheduler can pick up where left off, ensure that the simulation tick continues from saved tick, ensure that metrics are restored, ensure that the session state is sent to clients.

Potential issues: restoreSession currently sets simulation = Simulation.fromState(saved.simulation, content); but we need to ensure that the simulation's internal state is correctly restored (including tick). Also need to ensure that the thread IDs are resumed with codex (resume thread). Currently, after restoreSession, the server continues as normal: startTimers, etc. But we need to ensure that after restore, we need to resume threads and maybe reinitialize pending turns.

Also need to handle case where saved state exists but codex is not connected: need to reconnect and resume threads.

Potential failure handling: if saved state is corrupted (e.g., missing simulation), fallback to fresh start; if thread resume fails, treat as error and maybe reset.

Testing: need integration tests that simulate a saved session, restart server, verify that simulation continues, that pending turns are resumed, that metrics are restored, that clients receive correct session state.

We need to produce a plan with steps: 1) Add a method to resume threads after restore; 2) Modify restoreSession to handle errors; 3) Add a new method to load and resume threads; 4) Add tests.

Let's examine GameRuntimeServer's restoreSession:

```
private async restoreSession(): Promise<void> {
  const saved = await this.stateStore.load();
  if (!saved) {
    return;
  }

  const content = this.contentStore.getContent();
  if (saved.simulation) {
    this.simulation = Simulation.fromState(saved.simulation, content);
    this.seed = saved.simulation.seed;
    for (const agent of saved.simulation.agents ?? []) {
      this.agentConfigs.set(agent.id, { id: agent.id, name: agent.name });
    }
    this.resetAgentLoopState((saved.simulation.agents ?? []).map((agent) => agent.id));

  } else if (typeof saved.seed === "number") {
    this.simulation = new Simulation(saved.seed, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, content, [
      { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" }
    ]);
    this.seed = saved.seed;
    this.agentConfigs.set("agent-1", { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" });
    this.resetAgentLoopState(["agent-1"]);
  } else {
    return;
  }

  this.phase = "running";
  this.preparedSeed = null;
  this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} };
  this.threadToAgentId.clear();
  this.activeTurn = null;
  this.schedulerIndex = 0;
  await this.logReplay("session.restored", {
    seed: this.seed,
    tick: this.simulation.getState().tick
  });
}
```

Issues: It sets threadIds to empty, but saved.threadIds contains gameplayByAgentId and builder. It discards them. It also discards metrics and simulation state (except simulation). It also discards pending turns, which may be needed.

We need to restore threadIds from saved.threadIds, and resume threads with codex. Also need to restore metrics (maybe metrics are stored in saved.metrics). Also need to restore pending turns? The pending turns are for ongoing turn; after restore, we may have pending turn that was in progress; we need to handle that.

Also need to ensure that after restore, we need to resume threads (resume thread/resume codex). The codex client has a method to resume threads: we saw in reconnect: it calls request("thread/resume", { threadId }). So we need to call that for each threadId in saved.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId and builder.

Also need to ensure that we set this.threadIds = saved.threadIds (or at least copy). Also need to set threadToAgentId mapping accordingly.

Also need to ensure that we set this.agentConfigs appropriately: saved.simulation.agents may have agent configs; but we also need to set agentConfigs for each agent (maybe from saved.agentConfigs? There's no saved.agentConfigs; we have agentConfigs map that is built from simulation agents. In startSession we set agentConfigs from payload.agents. In restore, we set from saved.simulation.agents. That's okay.

But we also need to set this.threadToAgentId mapping: we can reconstruct from saved.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId.

Also need to set this.activeTurn? Might be null if no turn in progress; but if there was a pending turn, we need to handle it. The pendingTurns map is cleared in resetAgentLoopState; but we may want to preserve pending turn if it was in progress. However, after restore, we may have a pending turn that was interrupted? The saved state may have a pending turn (maybe not). The simulation state includes tick, but pending turns are not persisted. Perhaps we can ignore pending turns and let the scheduler pick up next turn.

But we need to ensure that pending turns are cleared to avoid stale references.

Also need to ensure that metrics are restored: saved.metrics contains runtime metrics; we need to set this.metrics = new MetricsTracker()? Actually MetricsTracker has a snapshot method that returns metrics; but we need to restore metrics state.

